# Error Handling — SmartPress

> Rewritten in Sprint 1.3. The previous version described a FastAPI backend's JSON error
> envelope for an FFmpeg video pipeline. Both are gone: the backend left with Sprint 1.1 and
> video with it. Every failure now happens on the user's own device.

The plan's cross-cutting rule is that **errors are typed UX states, never generic crashes or
silent failures**. The model that implements it lives in [`lib/errors.ts`](./lib/errors.ts).

---

## 1. The model

There is no HTTP status and no server, so a failure is a plain value:

```ts
interface AppError {
  code: ErrorCode;        // stable identifier
  message: string;        // what went wrong, in the user's terms
  remediation?: string;   // what they can do about it
  retryable: boolean;     // whether Retry could plausibly succeed unchanged
}
```

`retryable` drives the UI directly: the Retry button renders only when it is `true`. Offering
Retry on a file that will never decode is a lie the previous model told by omission — v2
declared the flag in its spec and never implemented it.

---

## 2. The codes

| Code | Cause | `retryable` | Surfaces as |
|---|---|---|---|
| `UNSUPPORTED_FORMAT` | Dropped file is not JPEG or PNG | no | Error row on add, before any work |
| `FILE_TOO_LARGE` | Input above `MAX_INPUT_BYTES` (100 MB) | no | Error row on add |
| `DECODE_FAILED` | `createImageBitmap` rejected — truncated, misnamed, or corrupt | no | Error row after the decode stage |
| `ENCODE_FAILED` | The wasm encoder threw | yes | Error row, with the raw detail appended |
| `CODEC_UNAVAILABLE` | A `/wasm/` binary did not load, or the format is disabled in this build | yes | Error row, scoped to that row |
| `OUT_OF_MEMORY` | Allocation failed decoding or encoding | yes | Error row |

`CODEC_UNAVAILABLE` covers AVIF: `CAPABILITIES.avif.available === false`, so nothing can
select it, and `encoders.ts` throws with the reason if something tries.

---

## 3. Classification crosses a worker boundary

Encoding runs in a worker, and `postMessage` cannot carry an `Error` instance — the worker
flattens it to `"${name}: ${message}"`. So `classify()` matches on that **string**, not on
`instanceof`. Anything that changes an error message shape can therefore change its
classification silently.

That is not hypothetical. Sprint 1.3 changed the wasm loader to build absolute URLs (see
§5), which turned `Failed to load /wasm/x.wasm` into
`Failed to load http://host/wasm/x.wasm` and dropped a `CODEC_UNAVAILABLE` into the generic
`ENCODE_FAILED` bucket until the matcher was widened to `/wasm/`. **Test the classification,
not just the throw.**

Order matters inside `classify()`: memory exhaustion surfaces through several unrelated
messages and is checked before the generic buckets can claim it.

---

## 4. Cancellation is not a failure

Removing a row or clearing the queue terminates the worker running that file, because a wasm
encode has no yield point to check a flag. The pool rejects the caller's promise with
`CancelledError`, and the UI checks `isCancelled(e)` and returns without rendering anything
— the row it belonged to no longer exists.

A cancelled job must still **settle** its promise. Before Sprint 1.3 the pool terminated the
worker and left the caller awaiting forever.

---

## 5. What "no network" means for errors

There are no request failures to model, with one exception: the vendored `.wasm` binaries are
fetched from `/wasm/` on the same origin at first use of a format. That fetch is the only
network call the app makes after load, and its failure is `CODEC_UNAVAILABLE` — scoped to the
row that needed it, never a global blocking state. A JPEG still compresses when the PNG
binary is missing.

A failed load does **not** poison the cache: `loader.ts` deletes the pending entry on
rejection, so Retry re-fetches rather than replaying the failure.
